Alright, let's dive into triangles! Even though we're in Algebra 2, understanding the basics of triangles is super important for many math topics, including trigonometry, which you'll definitely see.

Here are some notes on essential triangle concepts:

---

### Triangles: Basic Concepts and Classifications

A **triangle** is a polygon with three sides and three angles. The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always $180^\circ$.

#### 1. Classification by Side Lengths

Triangles can be classified based on the lengths of their sides:

*   **Equilateral Triangle:**
    *   All three sides are equal in length.
    *   All three angles are equal, each measuring $60^\circ$.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (1.5, 2.598); % 3*sin(60)
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
\node at (A) [below left] {A};
\node at (B) [below right] {B};
\node at (C) [above] {C};
\draw (A) ++(0.3,0) arc (0:60:0.3);
\draw (B) ++(-0.3,0) arc (180:120:0.3);
\draw (C) ++(-0.15,-0.2598) arc (-90:210:0.3); % Angle at C
\node at (A) [below right, xshift=-0.1cm, yshift=0.1cm] {$60^\circ$};
\node at (B) [below left, xshift=0.1cm, yshift=0.1cm] {$60^\circ$};
\node at (C) [above, yshift=-0.1cm] {$60^\circ$};
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, below] {$s$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {$s$};
\draw (C) -- (A) node[midway, left] {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

*   **Isosceles Triangle:**
    *   At least two sides are equal in length.
    *   The angles opposite the equal sides (called base angles) are also equal.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2, 3);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
\node at (A) [below left] {A};
\node at (B) [below right] {B};
\node at (C) [above] {C};
\draw (A) ++(0.3,0) arc (0:56.3:0.3); % Angle at A
\draw (B) ++(-0.3,0) arc (180:123.7:0.3); % Angle at B
\node at (A) [below right, xshift=-0.1cm, yshift=0.1cm] {$\alpha$};
\node at (B) [below left, xshift=0.1cm, yshift=0.1cm]

