### Geometry Diagram: A Right Triangle

Here's a simple right triangle. Remember, a right triangle is a triangle with one angle measuring $90^\circ$.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (4,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (0,3);

    % Draw the triangle
    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

    % Label the vertices
    \node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
    \node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
    \node[above left] at (C) {$C$};

    % Mark the right angle at A
    \draw (A) rectangle +(0.3,0.3);

    % Label the sides (optional, but good for notes)
    \node[midway, below] at (A) -- (B) {$b$}; % Base
    \node[midway, left] at (A) -- (C) {$a$};  % Height
    \node[midway, above right] at (B) -- (C) {$c$}; % Hypotenuse
\end{tikzpicture}
```

In this diagram:
*   The vertices are labeled $A$, $B$, and $C$.
*   The angle at vertex $A$ is a right angle ($90^\circ$).
*   The sides opposite the vertices are labeled with lowercase letters: side $a$ is opposite vertex $A$, side $b$ is opposite vertex $B$, and side $c$ (the hypotenuse) is opposite vertex $C$.

