Excellent idea! A clearly labeled right triangle is fundamental for understanding trigonometry. We'll use TikZ to create a diagram that shows the **hypotenuse**, **opposite**, and **adjacent** sides relative to an angle $\theta$.

---

### Trigonometric Right Triangle

Here's a right triangle labeled for trigonometry. Remember, these labels (opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse) are always defined *relative to a specific acute angle* in the triangle.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
    % Vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (4,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (0,3);

    % Draw the triangle
    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

    % Label the vertices
    \node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
    \node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
    \node[above left] at (C) {$C$};

    % Mark the right angle at A
    \draw (A) rectangle +(0.3,0.3);

    % Mark the angle theta at B
    \draw (B) arc (180:150:0.5);
    \node[below left] at (B) {$\theta$};

    % Label the sides relative to angle theta
    \node[midway, above right, sloped] at (B) -- (C) {\textbf{Hypotenuse}};
    \node[midway, left] at (A) -- (C) {\textbf{Opposite}};
    \node[midway, below] at (A) -- (B) {\textbf{Adjacent}};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

In this diagram:

*   The angle at vertex $A$ is the **right angle** ($90^\circ$).
*   We've chosen the angle at vertex $B$ as our reference angle, labeled as $\theta$ (theta).
*   **Hypotenuse:** This is the longest side of the right triangle, and it's always the side directly opposite the right angle. In our diagram, it's side $BC$.
*   **Opposite:** This is the side directly across from the reference angle $\theta$. In our diagram, it's side $AC$.
*   **Adjacent:** This is the side next to the reference angle $\theta$ that is *not* the hypotenuse. In our diagram, it's side $AB$.

These labels are crucial for defining the basic trigonometric ratios: sine, cosine, and tangent!
